  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Estar: the other \"to be\"",
      "body": "Spanish has two verbs for \"to be\". You know *ser*. The second is *estar*, which is irregular in the *yo* form and has accents on most forms.",
      "table": {
        "head": ["Pronoun", "estar"],
        "rows": [
          ["*yo*", "*estoy*"],
          ["*tú*", "*estás*"],
          ["*él / ella / usted*", "*está*"],
          ["*nosotros / nosotras*", "*estamos*"],
          ["*ustedes*", "*están*"],
          ["*ellos / ellas*", "*están*"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": "Use 1: where something is",
      "body": "Location — of people and things — uses *estar*: *¿Dónde está el baño?* (Where is the bathroom?) *Estoy en casa.* (I'm at home.) *Lima está en Perú.* (Lima is in Peru.)\n\n(One exception you'll learn later: for where an *event* takes place, like a party or a concert, Spanish uses *ser*.)"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Use 2: how someone or something is right now",
      "body": "States and conditions — how you feel, temporary situations — use *estar*: *Estoy cansado.* (I'm tired.) *Ella está contenta.* (She's happy.) *La sopa está fría.* (The soup is cold.) *La puerta está abierta.* (The door is open.)\n\nThat's why \"How are you?\" is *¿Cómo estás?*: it asks about your state today."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Ser or estar? A first rule of thumb",
      "body": "- *Ser*: what something **is** — identity, origin, nationality, job, personality, what it's like in general. *Ana es alta. Es de Chile. Es médica.*\n- *Estar*: where it **is**, and how it **is at the moment**. *Ana está en el hospital. Está cansada.*\n\nCompare: *Carlos es aburrido* (Carlos is a boring person) vs. *Carlos está aburrido* (Carlos is bored right now). You'll refine this over the next levels."
    }
  ],
